{"id":"pd-a-apple-pie","slug":"a-apple-pie","title":"A Apple Pie","authors":["Kate Greenaway"],"illustrators":["Kate Greenaway"],"translators":[],"description":"Kate Greenaway’s alphabet picture book — A was an apple pie, and every letter after it brings another crowd of children to the table.","language":"en","ageRange":"3-5","readingLevel":"pre-reader","estimatedMinutes":3,"categories":["picture-books","poetry-rhymes","short-reads"],"tags":["alphabet","kate greenaway","rhymes"],"cover":"library/a-apple-pie/cover.webp","thumbnail":"library/a-apple-pie/thumbnail.webp","pages":[{"number":1,"label":"PUBLISHER'S NOTE","text":"Kate Greenaway used an early version of the rhyme to illustrate A APPLE PIE which was first published in 1886 and it will be noticed that there is no rhyme for the letter I.\n\nThe rhyme of A APPLE PIE is very ancient and reference is made to it as early as 1671 in one of the writings of John Eachard. In these early versions the letters I and J were not differentiated. The letter J as we know it to-day was the curved initial form of the letter I and was always used before a vowel.","layout":{"kind":"text","fit":"contain","textPosition":"below"}},{"number":2,"label":"KATE GREENAWAY","text":"London FREDERICK WARNE & Ltd. & New York\n\nPrinted in Great Britain by W & J Mackay Limited, Chatham from original woodblock designs engraved in 1886","layout":{"kind":"text","fit":"contain","textPosition":"below"}},{"number":3,"image":"library/a-apple-pie/pages/001.webp","alt":"Illustration on title page","layout":{"kind":"picture","fit":"contain","textPosition":"none"}},{"number":4,"image":"library/a-apple-pie/pages/002.webp","alt":"A APPLE PIE","layout":{"kind":"picture","fit":"contain","textPosition":"none"}},{"number":5,"image":"library/a-apple-pie/pages/003.webp","alt":"B BIT IT","layout":{"kind":"picture","fit":"contain","textPosition":"none"}},{"number":6,"image":"library/a-apple-pie/pages/004.webp","alt":"C CUT IT","layout":{"kind":"picture","fit":"con